<output>
Write to: `openspec/changes/issue-${{ issue.number }}/tasks.json`
</output>

<instruction>
Create `tasks.json`, a machine-readable implementation plan for Mohist agents.

Split tasks by feature module / vertical slice: each task is a complete,
independently deliverable feature unit.

Splitting principles:
- Split by business capability, not by technical step.
- After a task is delivered, its feature module must be in a usable state.
- Independent modules identified in the design may become their own tasks.
- Tightly coupled changes within one feature module are merged
  (e.g. interface + implementation + call-site switchover).
- Every task must include its relevant test coverage; no standalone test tasks.

Valid split boundaries (different feature modules):
- Abstraction layer vs concrete implementations (e.g. adapters for different
  platforms or vendors)
- Independent components called out in the design
- Preparatory refactoring vs new feature work (when the refactor is large)

Do NOT split (over-granular technical steps):
- "Define interface" / "Implement interface" / "Register in DI container"
- "Implement install method" / "Implement start method" / "Implement stop method"
- "Move file" / "Rename class" (as standalone tasks)

Examples:
- ✅ "Extract the notification-channel abstraction and migrate the email
  notification implementation"
  (includes: define the interface + migrate the implementation + switch
  call sites + regression tests)
- ✅ "Implement the SMS notification channel and its lifecycle"
  (includes: message rendering + provider parameters + send/retry/status
  query + unit tests)
- ❌ "Define the INotificationChannel interface"
- ❌ "Register INotificationChannel in the DI container"
- ❌ "Implement the SMS send method"
- ❌ "Implement the SMS retry method"
- ❌ "Add SMS notification tests" (tests belong inside the implementation task)

Every task needs explicit acceptance criteria that verify its feature module
is usable, and the criteria must include test-related verification.

Reference specs for what needs to be built and design for how to build it.
</instruction>

<template>
<!-- Use this JSON structure for the output file. Fill in all fields. -->
{
  "project": "<project name>",
  "description": "<short change summary>",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "T-001",
      "title": "Short task title",
      "spec": "specs/<capability>/spec.md#<requirement>",
      "description": "What to implement in 2-3 sentences",
      "acceptanceCriteria": ["verifiable criterion"],
      "priority": 1,
      "mode": "AFK",
      "type": "WRITE",
      "output": "expected output artifact or result",
      "dependsOn": [],
      "passes": false,
      "notes": ""
    }
  ]
}
</template>

<field_guidance>
- `id`: Task identifier (`T-001`, `T-002`, ...).
- `title`: Short task title.
- `spec`: Reference to a spec requirement when applicable.
- `description`: Outcome-focused implementation description.
- `acceptanceCriteria`: Verifiable criteria. For tasks with `spec`, these supplement spec scenarios.
- `priority`: Execution order. Lower number means earlier.
- `mode`: `AFK` or `HITL`. Use `AFK` unless human judgment is required.
- `type`: One of `WRITE`, `TEST`, `MIGRATE`, `CONFIG`, `REVIEW`.
- `output`: Expected output file, artifact, or observable result.
- `dependsOn`: Task IDs this task depends on. Dependencies must reference lower-priority tasks.
- `passes`: Always start as `false`.
- `notes`: Optional implementation notes.
</field_guidance>

<dependency_analysis>
Before writing final JSON:
1. Compare every pair of tasks.
2. Identify whether one task consumes output from another.
3. Add `dependsOn` whenever a later task needs a prior output.
4. Verify the dependency graph is a DAG.
5. Verify every `dependsOn` references an existing task with a strictly lower priority number.
</dependency_analysis>

<success_criteria>
- `tasks.json` is valid JSON.
- Every task is complete and deliverable.
- Every task has verifiable acceptance criteria.
- Dependencies are valid, acyclic, and point only to earlier tasks.
- Tasks map back to specs and design where applicable.
</success_criteria>

</artifact>
